<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Apollonius Molon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Molon of Rhodes</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Apollonius Molon was a Greek rhetorician from Alabanda in Caria who taught on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a> in the early first century BCE. He visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as an ambassador of Rhodes in 81 BCE, and among his pupils were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>, who studied with him on Rhodes in 78 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>. He favoured a moderate style between the plain Attic and the florid Asian manners.</p></div>
